<header class="page-hero">
  <h1>Safety is not a premium option.</h1>
  <p class="page-hero-subtitle">
    skyBlip helps you see and be seen. For recreational flying and air sports,
    its safety features will always be open source and free.
  </p>
</header>

<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">The problem</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Mid-air collisions remain among the most serious accidents in light
    aviation, and among the most avoidable. Making yourself visible has been
    possible for years. But it is expensive, the systems do not talk to each
    other, and it often requires a subscription.
  </p>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>Affordable.</h3>
      <p>Equipment only helps if it is on board. We use mass-produced consumer hardware. For recreational flying and air sports, the firmware and updates stay free.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Open source.</h3>
      <p>The code that broadcasts your position, receives traffic data and sounds the alarm is public. Anyone can read it, check it and fix it.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Compatible.</h3>
      <p>ADS-L is the open standard published by EASA. skyBlip also receives FLARM traffic, rather than going its own way.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">The vision: <span class="highlight">every flight visible to all</span></h2>
  <ol class="steps-grid">
    <li>
      <h3>Aircraft to aircraft.</h3>
      <p>Two skyBlip Go devices see each other with no infrastructure at all. Every ADS-L transmitter makes the sky easier to read for the others.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>With the gliders.</h3>
      <p>They already fly with FLARM, tracked by the Open Glider Network (OGN). skyBlip receives their signals, and skyPost stations will exchange data with OGN.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>ADS&#8209;B on board.</h3>
      <p><%= link_to_page "skyblip-bravo", label: "Bravo" %> pairs with a Go to form a <%= link_to_page "skyblip-tango", label: "Tango" %> and adds airliners to the ADS-L and FLARM traffic shown. A pilot who buys a Tango to see them is also seen in ADS-L.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Every flight.</h3>
      <p>From light aircraft to paramotors, broadcast your position in ADS-L. Not because it is mandatory, but because it is useful from the first flight.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="more"><%= link_to_page "roadmap", label: "What comes next" %></p>
</section>

<section class="band band--blip quote">
  <blockquote>
    &ldquo;For recreational flying and air sports, position broadcasts, traffic reception, alarms and
